; generated-format instantiation 2026-08-08
|term[path]
  :status settled
  :subject-field addressing
  :layer framing-vocabulary
  :gloss One set of monotonic steps between an origin location and a destination location.
  :see-also [@sequence-cause @universal-location]
  |designation path
    :status preferred
    :channel prose
  |definition
    :defined-by @def-locations-and-paths
    :anchor path
    :projection paraphrase
    A PATH is one set of possibly many (so, not necessarily unique) monotonic steps between an origin LOCATION and a destination LOCATION.
  |formal
    :notation <latex:p: loc_{org} \to loc_{dst} = \langle s_1, ..., s_n \rangle>
    :reading Each step's imperative-vs-declarative character derives from its sequence-cause, per segment.
  ; WHY one `origin` relation with :one-of, not three relation kinds: the
  ; trichotomy qualifies the MANNER of the single origin seat; three kinds
  ; would triple the seat and make the {1,1} arity inexpressible.
  |rel :kind origin :to @location :card 1 :one-of [explicit implicit universal]
  |rel :kind destination :to @location :card 1
  |rel :kind has :to @sequence-cause :card +
  |invariant
    The origin is exactly one of explicit | implicit | universal (steward sharpening 2026-08-08; body-absorption candidate recorded in def-locations-and-paths WN).
  |not
    Not necessarily unique, reversible, or shortest; logical by default — physical equivalence is by construction and intentional.
